In Siberia, cracks in the ground poured out lava again and again. The lava piles, called the Siberian Traps, still cover an area several times the size of Maharashtra. Like India's later [[@deccan|Deccan Traps]], they formed as layer upon layer of flat sheets, not as a single cone-shaped volcano.

The lava itself was not the main killer. The eruptions released huge amounts of [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]] and other gases. Some lava also baked through thick layers of coal and other rock underground, adding even more gas. Very precise dating suggests that most of the dying happened within about 60,000 years. On the scale of Earth's history, that is a blink.

{{story:climate-change}}

Jakarta, the capital of Indonesia, sits on soft coastal land. Millions of people rely on private wells, and parts of the north of the city have sunk several metres over the past few decades. Sea walls now guard neighbourhoods that are lower than the water beside them.

Indonesia's government decided in 2019 to build a new capital, Nusantara, on the island of Borneo. Jakarta's sinking and crowding were among the reasons. Engineers are also building bigger sea walls and asking people to switch to piped water.

{{story:groundwater}}

Add up everything we can see: stars, planets, gas, people, sandwiches. It comes to only about 5% of the universe. About 27% is [[@mystery-dark-matter|dark matter]], invisible stuff whose gravity holds galaxies together. And about 68% seems to be dark energy.

So nearly everything in the cosmos is made of things we've never directly detected. It's like discovering that the books you can read are only one shelf in a library the size of a city.

{{story:mystery-dark-matter}}

Air rushing in at over 300 km/h could make breathing hard. Peregrines have small bony cones in their nostrils that break up the blast of air. Some jet engines use a similar cone shape at the front to slow the air before it rushes in. People often point to the falcon as a natural example of the same idea.

To protect their eyes, peregrines use a third eyelid, a clear film that sweeps across each eye like a windscreen wiper. Their eyesight is several times sharper than ours, good enough to pick out a single pigeon in a distant flock.

{{story:urban-wildlife}}

Rocky shores often have bands of life, like stripes on the rock. High up, where the sea only splashes, live animals that can survive long hours in the air, such as small periwinkle snails and some barnacles. Lower down, mussels crowd together. The lowest band, uncovered only at the lowest tides, has the most delicate animals, like soft sea anemones, starfish and sea urchins.

Some animals have clever tricks. Limpets clamp down so tightly that it is very hard to pull them off. Many limpets also return to the same spot on the rock after feeding, where their shell has worn a perfect fit. Barnacles start life as tiny swimming [[larva|larvae]], then [[@glue-sticky|glue]] their heads to a rock and build a shell around themselves. They spend the rest of their lives kicking food into their mouths with their legs.

{{story:moon-phases}}

In 1945, an American engineer named Percy Spencer was working at a company called Raytheon, which built radar sets. Radar uses a device called a magnetron to make powerful microwaves. As the story is usually told, Spencer was standing near a working magnetron when he noticed that a snack bar in his pocket had melted.

Curious, he tried pointing the microwaves at popcorn kernels, which [[@popcorn|popped]] all over the lab. Raytheon patented the idea and in 1947 sold the first microwave oven, called the Radarange. It was about 1.8 metres tall and very expensive, and was used mainly in restaurants, ships and railway dining cars. Small, cheap kitchen models came in the late 1960s and 1970s.

{{story:radio-waves}}

From the 1500s, Kathak was performed at Mughal courts, as well as [[@rajputs|Rajput courts]] in Rajasthan. Court life changed it. Dancers wore long, flared *angarkha* coats and caps instead of temple costumes. Persian poetry and court manners shaped new pieces.

Different centres grew their own styles, called *gharanas*. In Lucknow, the court of Nawab Wajid Ali Shah, in the 1800s, prized grace and expression. The [[@jaipur|Jaipur]] gharana became known for powerful footwork and spins. A third school grew in Banaras.

{{story:mughals}}

After a great fire in 1871, Chicago rebuilt quickly with tall office buildings on steel frames. The Home Insurance Building there, finished in 1885, is often called the first [[@skyscrapers|skyscraper]].

Tall buildings only worked because of another invention, the safety [[@elevators|elevator]]. Without it, nobody would rent an office on the 20th floor. Soon cities like Chicago and New York were racing upwards.

{{story:elevators}}

The story told by Manjhi and his family goes like this. His wife, Falguni Devi, was crossing the ridge with water or food for him when she slipped and was badly hurt. Some accounts say she later died because help was too far away. Others say she fell ill and died some years after.

Whatever the exact order, Manjhi decided nobody else should suffer because of that ridge. He sold his goats to buy tools. Then he started hitting the rock.

{{story:lever-lift}}

Indian mathematicians were working on similar ideas, and in some ways went further. In 628 CE, Brahmagupta wrote rules for calculating with zero and with [[@negative-numbers|negative numbers]], which he called debts. He also solved equations with more than one unknown.

To name unknowns, Indian mathematicians used the first [[syllable|syllables]] of words. The first unknown was often *yavat-tavat*, meaning "as much as". For more unknowns, writers such as Brahmagupta and, later, Bhaskara II used colour names: *kalaka* (black), *nilaka* (blue), *pitaka* (yellow) and so on. A problem might have a black number and a blue number to find, which is not so different from x and y.

{{story:great-libraries}}

Count one number every second, with no sleep or meals. You reach a thousand in about 17 minutes and a lakh in about 28 hours. A crore takes almost four months.

A billion takes about 32 years. A child who started on her eighth birthday would finish at around 40. That gap is the clearest way to see how much bigger each step is. Every time you add a zero, the counting takes ten times longer, which is why [[@zero-invent|zero]] was such a powerful invention.

{{story:light-years}}

Disney animators Frank Thomas and Ollie Johnston wrote down twelve principles of animation in their 1981 book. One is "squash and stretch": a bouncing ball flattens when it hits the ground and stretches as it flies. Real balls barely do this, but the exaggeration makes the movement feel alive.

Another is timing. More drawings close together make a movement slow and smooth. Fewer, spaced far apart, make it snappy. A character who blinks in two frames looks alert, while one who takes eight looks sleepy. Good animators study real people and animals for hours, because [[@movies-move|movement on film]] looks natural only when it is cleverly faked.

{{story:movies-move}}

In the Braj region around Mathura and Vrindavan, where Krishna is believed to have grown up, Holi lasts more than a week. A popular story says the [[@myths-krishna-childhood|young Krishna]], who had dark blue skin, worried that fair Radha would not like him. His mother told him to colour Radha's face any shade he wished.

In Barsana, linked with Radha, women hold Lathmar Holi, playfully hitting men with long sticks called lathis. In Vrindavan's temples, priests throw flower petals instead of powder. Pilgrims come from across India to join in.

{{story:seasons-why}}

Researchers at University College London asked children to taste a vegetable they did not like, in tiny amounts, on eight days spread over about two weeks. Many children ended up liking it more. Other studies suggest children often need 8 to 15 tries before they accept a new food.

Forcing children to eat usually backfires. Tasting without pressure, and seeing parents and friends enjoy the food, works better. This is called "learning to like". It explains why foods that seemed awful at six can taste lovely at sixteen. Cooking helps too. Roasting cauliflower or frying bhindi until crisp brings out sweeter, nuttier flavours that hide some of the bitterness.

{{story:taste-smell-link}}

Each "G" brought a new trick. 1G, in the 1980s, carried voice calls as analogue signals, and anyone with the right radio could listen in. 2G, in the 1990s, turned calls digital and added text messages. 3G made the internet usable on a phone. 4G, around 2010, made it fast enough for [[@tech-video-calls|video calls]] and streaming.

{{story:mobile-networks}}

5G is built for a world where phones are only part of the story. [[sensor|Sensors]], cars, factory robots and smart meters all want to connect too, and 5G can handle far more devices in the same area.

The answer was the "safety bicycle". Starley's Rover of 1885 had two wheels of similar size and a chain linking the pedals to the back wheel. [[@gears|Gears]] did the job the giant wheel had done, and the rider sat low enough to put a foot down.

In 1888, John Boyd Dunlop, a Scottish vet living in Belfast, made air-filled rubber tyres for his young son's tricycle so it would ride more smoothly. Air tyres made cycling far more comfortable. A bicycle craze swept Europe and the United States in the 1890s.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

Take a smart thermostat, which controls heating or cooling. A temperature sensor measures the room many times a minute. Its chip compares the reading to the setting you chose. If the room is too warm, it switches on the air conditioner. It also sends the data to an app, so you can change the setting from across town.

The heavy work often happens in [[@cloud-computing|cloud computing]] centres, big buildings full of servers far away. Your gadget stays small and cheap, and the clever bits run elsewhere.

{{story:smart-cities}}

In 1995 the United States granted a patent to two researchers for using [[@turmeric|turmeric]] to help heal wounds. India's Council of Scientific and Industrial Research challenged it. It showed old Sanskrit texts and a 1953 article from an Indian medical journal proving the use was already known. In 1997 the patent was cancelled.

{{story:turmeric}}

A similar fight followed over a European patent on a fungicide made from the [[@neem|neem]] tree, which was later revoked too. To stop this happening again, India built a huge digital library of traditional medicine, translated into several languages, so patent examiners around the world can check it.

In the 1990s and 2000s, researchers tried a new approach. Instead of writing rules, they fed computers millions of sentences already translated by people, such as United Nations reports, and let them count which phrases matched up. Google Translate launched with this method in 2006.

It worked better, but translations often came out choppy, because the system pieced together short phrases without seeing the whole sentence.

{{story:languages-india}}

Gutenberg's breakthrough was putting several ideas together. He invented a hand mould that could quickly cast many identical metal letters. He mixed an ink that stuck well to metal. He used a screw press to push paper evenly onto the inked type.

His most famous book, the Gutenberg Bible, was finished around 1455. About 180 copies were printed, some on [[@paper-invention|paper]] and some on vellum made from calfskin. Gutenberg himself did not grow rich. He lost a lawsuit to his business partner and lost much of his workshop.

{{story:printing-press}}

In 1931, work began on a huge dam across the Colorado River, about 50 kilometres away. Thousands of workers came looking for jobs during hard times. The same year, Nevada made gambling legal. Workers with wages in their pockets, and tourists who came to see the dam, found casinos and shows waiting in town.

The [[@hoover-dam|Hoover Dam]] was finished in 1936. Behind it, a lake called Lake Mead filled up. The dam also produced electricity, which helped power the city's lights and air conditioners.

{{story:hoover-dam}}

In 1607, English settlers founded Jamestown in Virginia, and English took root in North America. Over the next three centuries, the British Empire carried the language around the globe. In India, the [[@east-india-company|East India Company]] began trading in 1600 and slowly became a ruling power. In 1835, the official Thomas Macaulay persuaded the government to spend money on English-language education, to train Indians who could work for the British.

That policy was controversial then and is still argued about now. But Indians made English their own. Leaders of the [[@freedom-struggle|freedom struggle]] wrote and argued in it, and writers such as [[@rk-narayan|R. K. Narayan]] and [[@ruskin-bond|Ruskin Bond]] won readers all over the world.

{{story:internet-history}}

A traditional Japanese breakfast looks like a small lunch: a bowl of rice, miso soup, a piece of grilled fish, pickled vegetables and sometimes natto, sticky fermented [[@soybeans|soybeans]] with a strong smell. Many busy families in [[@japan-culture|Japan]] now eat toast too.

China has congee, a soft rice porridge, often eaten with youtiao, long fried dough sticks. In Vietnam, many people start the day with pho, a noodle soup. Soup for breakfast is common across much of Asia.

{{story:body-clock}}

In 1938 the [[archaeologist|archaeologist]] Luther Cressman dug in Fort Rock Cave in Oregon. Under a layer of volcanic ash, he found dozens of sandals woven from shredded sagebrush bark. They had flat soles and flaps that folded over the toes.

{{story:stone-age}}

Years later, radiocarbon dating showed the sandals were about 9,000 to 10,000 years old. They are still the oldest shoes known. The dry cave and the ash had protected them for thousands of years, the kind of lucky find [[@archaeology|archaeology]] depends on.
